the word be a palindrome for arbitrarily large , meaning for every . Then is either quadratic irrational or transcendental. For a palindromic prefix, the product of the symmetric matrices is symmetric, because transposition reverses the factors. By <a data-href="Recurrence and matrix formula for convergents" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/recurrence-and-matrix-formula-for-convergents.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Recurrence and matrix formula for convergents</a>, Here the rows are swapped from the standard because the continued fraction has and we removed it from the product. Hence .
<br>The <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Fundamental identity for continued fraction convergents" data-href="Fundamental identity for continued fraction convergents" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/fundamental-identity-for-continued-fraction-convergents.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">determinant identity</a> gives . Since , Also .
<br>These common-denominator approximations hold for unbounded . Choose ; eventually . Since is irrational, <a data-href="Transcendence from simultaneous approximation to a number and its square" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/transcendence-from-simultaneous-approximation-to-a-number-and-its-square.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Transcendence from simultaneous approximation to a number and its square</a> gives the conclusion unless is quadratic. Note No growth hypothesis. The argument does not require bounded partial quotients.
<br>Attribution. See <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="https://adamczewski.perso.math.cnrs.fr/FrContPal.pdf" rel="noopener nofollow" class="external-link is-unresolved" href="https://adamczewski.perso.math.cnrs.fr/FrContPal.pdf" target="_self">Adamczewski–Bugeaud, Palindromic continued fractions, Theorem 1</a>.

Let be represented by two infinite admissible digit sequences for <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Jacobi-Perron algorithm" data-href="Jacobi-Perron algorithm" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/jacobi-perron-algorithm.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Jacobi's algorithm</a>;
<br> be the <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Convergents of Jacobi multidimensional continued fractions" data-href="Convergents of Jacobi multidimensional continued fractions" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/convergents-of-jacobi-multidimensional-continued-fractions.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">convergent data</a>;
, and for every sufficiently large , Then and are transcendental, and are linearly independent over . <br>Fix either coordinate , with numerator sequence . By <a data-href="Approximation bounds for Jacobi convergents" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/approximation-bounds-for-jacobi-convergents.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Approximation bounds for Jacobi convergents</a>, infinitely many indices satisfy <br>Reduce these fractions to with . The same errors are less than . Because is irrational and the errors tend to zero, infinitely many distinct reduced fractions occur. <a data-href="Roth approximation theorem" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/roth-approximation-theorem.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Roth approximation theorem</a> implies transcendence.
<br>The independence assertion is the standard rational-relations property of an uninterrupted two-dimensional Jacobi expansion, stated in <a data-href="Jacobi-Perron algorithm" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/jacobi-perron-algorithm.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Jacobi-Perron algorithm</a>. Note
This is the criterion of <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="https://arxiv.org/html/2505.03384v1#S5" rel="noopener nofollow" class="external-link is-unresolved" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2505.03384v1#S5" target="_self">Accossato–Murru–Romeo, Theorem 19</a>. The hypothesis must hold eventually at all indices, so it applies to whichever subsequences give the two approximation bounds. The adjacent minors can be computed before choosing , since the terms cancel: Thus choose , compute both minors, and choose the integer strictly larger than , , and the two displayed growth bounds. This enforces admissibility and yields a pair covered by the theorem.

Let and be a nonterminating <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Ruban continued fraction algorithm" data-href="Ruban continued fraction algorithm" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/ruban-continued-fraction-algorithm.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Ruban</a> or <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="Browkin continued fraction algorithm" data-href="Browkin continued fraction algorithm" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/browkin-continued-fraction-algorithm.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Browkin I</a> expansion;
, , , ; and . Then for , while ;
the convergents satisfy in . <br> The first case is . Since every , induction makes the valuation of strictly smaller than that of . Equality in the <a data-tooltip-position="top" aria-label="p-adic valuation" data-href="p-adic valuation" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/p-adic-valuation.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">ultrametric inequality</a> gives the formula. The matrix and determinant identities are algebraic and remain valid for the rational digits . Hence Since , the term has strictly smaller valuation than . Thus Note
The proof uses -adic valuations, not real positivity or increasing real denominators. The uppercase distinguish these rational recurrence values from reduced integer numerators and denominators.

