Let . Then has a finite <a data-href="Simple continued fraction" href="./https://alphaprivativa.github.io/Group-Cohomology-Bachelor-Thesis/main_HTML_ENG/simple-continued-fraction.html" class="internal-link" target="_self" rel="noopener nofollow">Simple continued fraction</a> expansion;
it has a unique canonical expansion with final partial quotient when ; an integer has canonical expansion ;
the other finite simple expansion is obtained by
with the integer case . Write with . Euclidean division gives , . If , stop; otherwise replace by and repeat. The positive remainders strictly decrease. At every nonterminal step is the floor of the current complete quotient. The next quotient is forced by reciprocal subtraction, so the procedure is unique. Its final integer is greater than unless the input was already an integer. The identity produces the second expansion. Conversely, a terminal can be absorbed into the preceding term; every other expansion is forced by the same floor procedure. Example
. Warning
The ban on a final applies only when there is more than one term. The integer still has canonical expansion .

